## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design for real-time monocular 6-DoF pose with few-shot adaptation on edge hardware, including data strategy and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Lightweight backbone plus specialized heads for detection, segmentation, and pose
- On-device few-shot adaptation via adapters or compact fine-tuning
- Synthetic+real data and domain randomization
- Occlusion robustness and reprojection-based losses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: simple pose reprojection loss sketch
function poseLoss(pred, gt, intr) {
  // pred: 6-DoF [rx,ry,rz,tx,ty,tz], gt: ground truth
  // project 3D corners to 2D and compare
  const pts3D = corners3D();
  const pts2D = project(pts3D, pred, intr);
  return meanSquaredError(pts2D, gt2D);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure adaptation effectiveness on unseen feature types with minimal labeling?
- What strategies mitigate drift when features are tiny or heavily occluded?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to handle equipment with strong reflective/transparent surfaces, a realistic industrial setting, and on-device adaptation under tight latency budgets.

## Key Concepts
- Reflective/transparent object pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for photometric consistency
- On-device 3-shot adaptation via compact adapters
- Synthetic+real data with material randomization
- Edge latency and robustness under glare

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch
function forward(frame) {
  const feats = backbone(frame);
  const seg = segHead(feats);
  const pose = poseHead(feats);
  const render = differentiableRenderer(pose, frame);
  return {seg, pose, loss: computeLoss(render, frame)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to varying refractive indices?
- How would you quantify uncertainty in pose under specular highlights?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical, beginner-friendly CV pipeline for real-time counting under occlusion and lighting changes. Emphasizes fallbacks to simple cues and a tiny classifier, plus lightweight tracking for stability.

## Key Concepts
- ROI extraction and scene normalization
- Blob-based counting from simple cues
- Lightweight classifier for type refinement
- Frame-to-frame tracking (Kalman)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: basic blob counting and simple classifier usage
function countItems(frameROI, types) {
  const blobs = segmentByColorAndEdges(frameROI);
  const counts = {};
  for (const t of types) counts[t] = 0;
  for (const b of blobs) {
    const type = classifyBlob(b, t); // tiny CNN or heuristic
    if (type) counts[type] += 1;
  }
  return counts;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new product types without retraining the classifier?
- What are failure modes with reflective packaging or transparent wrappers?

